    "https://ollusa.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204895": {
      "title": "Director of Alumni Relations, Community and Family Engagement",
      "description": "Our Lady of the Lake University\nJob Posting: Director of Alumni Relations, Community and Family Engagement Our Lady of the Lake University \n\tJob Posting: Director of Alumni Relations, Community and Family Engagement Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director of Alumni Relations, Community and Family Engagement Category: Staff Full Time Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Institutional Advancement Locations: San Antonio, TX Posted: Sep 16, 2026 Closes: Oct 16, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Position ID: 204895 Share About Our Lady of the Lake University: On a foundation of faith, wisdom and trust in Divine Providence, Our Lady of the Lake University (OLLU) empowers graduates to transform their communities, families, and professions. Founded in 1895 by the Sisters of Divine Providence, OLLU stands as San Antonio's oldest regionally accredited institution of higher learning. A private, Catholic university, it currently enrolls approximately 1,800 students across undergraduate and graduate programs. With its main residential campus in San Antonio and additional locations in Houston and the Rio Grande Valley (La Feria), and online programs, OLLU extends its mission across Texas, the border region, and the nation. Academic Programs OLLU offers a rich academic portfolio of more than 30 undergraduate majors and minors, 14 master's programs and three doctoral programs. Courses are delivered through diverse formats to support traditional and non-traditional students alike. Job Description: Job Summary: The Director of Alumni Relations, Community and Family Engagement is a leadership role responsible for developing and executing a comprehensive engagement strategy for the university's constituents. The director serves as an ambassador and is charged with building and maintaining strong relationships with alumni, current and prospective families, and the broader community. This multifaceted position requires strong communication, leadership, and organizational skills. Works in a collaborative environment across the University, in close coordination with leadership in all academic areas to participate in programming, events, and identifying outstanding alumni, families and benefactors. Performance Evaluation Metrics Alumni, Family & Community Engagement: 1. Achieve a 10% year-over-year increase in alumni, family, and community engagement through events, programs, communications, volunteer opportunities, and other meaningful touchpoints. CRM Data & Engagement Tracking: 2. Ensure 95% of defined engagement activities and constituent updates are accurately and timely documented in Raiser's Edge, including event participation, interactions, referrals, updated contact information, and prospective donor opportunities. Engagement ROI & Pipeline Development: 3. Demonstrate measurable ROI from engagement activities by tracking participation, cost, constituent growth, prospect referrals, and philanthropic outcomes. Achieve a 10% annual increase in qualified prospects or referrals generated through engagement activities and documented in the CRM. Operational Excellence & Collaboration: 4. Achieve 90% satisfaction from key internal and external partners while meeting 90% of established project, event, and program timelines and goals. Essential Functions: Strategic planning and administration: Develop a multi-year, strategic plan for alumni relations, community and family engagement that aligns with the university's institutional goals and objectives. Manage and oversee the office's operating budget and programs, ensuring an appropriate return on investment for all activities. Track and analyze engagement metrics, using data to assess program effectiveness and inform future strategies. Use data and stakeholder feedback to assess needs and opportunities for process improvement and developing strategic action plans to achieve goals. Develop and implement strategies to increase alumni participation in annual giving and other philanthropic initiatives. Identify, qualify, cultivate, and refer alumni, families, and community members with philanthropic capacity and affinity. Develop engagement strategies that intentionally move constituents from engagement to philanthropy. Partner with major gifts, annual giving, advancement leadership, and development staff on constituent identification and cultivation. Identify prospective donors through alumni, family, and community engagement activities. Develop strategies for alumni and family giving societies. Track and report alumni giving participation, dollars raised, donor retention, upgrades, and referrals. Community and Family Engagement: Responsible for developing constituent engagement pathways through an alumni/community engagement lifecycle: Identify ? Connect ? Engage ? Involve ? Volunteer ? Advocate ? Give ? Renew/Upgrade. Build and strengthen relationships with a wide range of alumni and community leaders (including local school districts) both regionally and nationally. In coordination with the Office of Marketing, Communications and Engagement, implement communication and branding strategies?including email, social media, website, event messaging and e-newsletters and print?to keep constituents informed of university news, events, and accomplishments. Serve as a liaison between the university and its various constituencies, advocating for the institution's priorities. Work in a collaborative environment across the University, in close coordination with leadership in all areas such as admissions, career services, and athletics, to create engagement opportunities for students, families, and alumni.  Participate in programming, events, and identifying outstanding alumni, families and benefactors. Oversees Homecoming Weekend, reunions, and other alumni and family events on and off campus. Assists with Commencement events and coordinates related programming for alumni and graduates and their families. Support alumni giving and fundraising initiatives, including securing sponsorships and philanthropic support for alumni programs, scholarships, and engagement priorities in coordination with Institutional Advancement. Consistently input call reports, e-mail communications and meeting notes into university fundraising database. Identify and plan and/or coordinate events with alumni for the President. Create and cultivate opportunities for alumni and donors to support the university and build strategic partnerships with public and private sectors. Develop and maintain strategic partnerships that increase OLLU's visibility, enrollment pipeline, community impact, alumni engagement, and philanthropic opportunities. Event and Program Management: Conceive, plan, and execute a wide array of programs and special events, such as alumni reunions, Family Weekend, networking receptions, and fundraising events. Manage all logistics for events, including securing venues, arranging invitations and speakers, and managing on-site activities. Oversee volunteer management programs, recruiting and motivating volunteers to support alumni and family activities. Facilitate career networking services for students and alumni by leveraging the university's network of graduates. Provide leadership, supervision, and guidance to team members and volunteers. Ensures alignment with institutional mission and branding, cross networking and collaborating with internal departments/divisions to foster and design inclusive programs between alumni, families, and OLLU campus community bridging connections to student success to obtain a secondary education (Programs might include parent empowerment workshops, leadership development, and college readiness). Strategizes the implementation of a Family Program which serves as the first line of contact for families of current and former students and for campus partners working with parents. Oversees Alumni Executive Council (AEC) within context of their bylaws, committees, providing staff leadership, direction and support, identifying, recruiting, training and inspiring volunteers to engage with the university and with one another in areas of admissions, athletics, career employment services and regional programming. Building a pipeline for family engagement utilizing a targeted approach for both public and private schools especially in the surrounding OLLU regional area. Additional Responsibilities: Accepts and fulfills other duties as assigned by the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement as a team member in the overall activities of the University, including working some nights, weekends, occasional travel, and attending special events as necessary. Requirements: Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Excellent communication skills: Outstanding written, verbal, and interpersonal skills for interacting with diverse groups, including alumni, community leaders, donors, faculty, and administrators. Strategic thinking: Ability to develop a long-term strategic vision and convert it into actionable programming. Organizational skills: Strong project management abilities to coordinate multiple events and initiatives with attention to detail. Database and Technical proficiency: Familiarity with customer relationship management (CRM) systems. Working knowledge of Microsoft Office productivity. Relationship management: Demonstrated talent for recruiting, managing, and inspiring volunteers and building positive relationships with constituents-internal and external leadership, community leaders, donors etc. Creativity: Ability to develop new and innovative programs to increase constituent engagement. Flexibility: Willingness to work evenings and weekends and to travel as required for events. Integrity: Ability to maintain confidentiality and adhere to the University's core values of Community, Integrity, Trust and Service Education and Experience: A bachelor's degree is required, and a master's degree in a relevant field, such as communications, business management, marketing, or public relations, is often preferred. Experience in a higher education or non-profit environment is highly preferred. Requires at least five to seven years of progressively responsible experience in alumni relations, fundraising, or community and/or family engagement. Proven track record of building and executing strategic engagement initiatives is required. Requires a strong understanding of branding and alumni relations and community/family engagement. Strong relationship-building skills with the ability to engage a variety of stakeholders, including alumni, donors, and internal teams is required. Sophisticated communicator with an ability to influence decision makers. Highly responsible, self-motivated, and exceptional attention to detail. Bilingual (English-Spanish) preferred. Additional Information: Employment with Our Lady of the Lake University is contingent upon the applicant undergoing a background investigation. Our Lady of the Lake University uses E-Verify to confirm the employment eligibility of all newly hired employees. To learn more about E-Verify, including your rights and responsibilities, please visit www.dhs.gov/E-Verify. OLLU seeks to attract, develop and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administrators. The University is committed to diversity and strongly encourages applications from women and minorities. EOE Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Our Lady of the Lake University 411 S.W. 24th Street\n\t\t\t\t\tSan Antonio, Texas 78207"
    },
    "https://ollusa.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204897": {
      "title": "Director of Grant Development",
      "description": "Our Lady of the Lake University\nJob Posting: Director of Grant Development Our Lady of the Lake University \n\tJob Posting: Director of Grant Development Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director of Grant Development Category: Staff Full Time Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Institutional Advancement Locations: San Antonio, TX Posted: Sep 16, 2026 Closes: Oct 16, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Position ID: 204897 Share About Our Lady of the Lake University: On a foundation of faith, wisdom and trust in Divine Providence, Our Lady of the Lake University (OLLU) empowers graduates to transform their communities, families, and professions. Founded in 1895 by the Sisters of Divine Providence, OLLU stands as San Antonio's oldest regionally accredited institution of higher learning. A private, Catholic university, it currently enrolls approximately 1,800 students across undergraduate and graduate programs. With its main residential campus in San Antonio and additional locations in Houston and the Rio Grande Valley (La Feria), and online programs, OLLU extends its mission across Texas, the border region, and the nation. Academic Programs OLLU offers a rich academic portfolio of more than 30 undergraduate majors and minors, 14 master's programs and three doctoral programs. Courses are delivered through diverse formats to support traditional and non-traditional students alike. Job Description: Job Summary Reporting to the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement, the Director of Grant Development provides strategic leadership for the identification, cultivation, development, submission, tracking, stewardship, and renewal of grant opportunities that advance Our Lady of the Lake University's institutional priorities. The Director develops and manages a comprehensive grant strategy and pipeline encompassing private and corporate foundations, government agencies, and other institutional funding sources at the local, state, and federal levels. Working collaboratively with faculty, academic leadership, Finance, Institutional Advancement, and program teams, the Director translates University priorities into competitive and fundable proposals. The Director proactively develops relationships with institutional funders, identifies emerging funding opportunities, and utilizes AI, prospect research platforms, grant databases, and other technologies to strengthen funding intelligence, proposal development, and grant strategy. The position serves as the University's central resource for grant development and contributes directly to OLLU's overall fundraising and institutional advancement goals. Performance Evaluation Metrics 1. Grant Pipeline Growth Achieve a 10% year-over-year increase in qualified grant opportunities and proposals submitted that align with University priorities and Institutional Advancement fundraising goals. 2. Proposal Quality & Timeliness Ensure 95% of approved grant proposals, applications, and required reports are completed accurately and submitted by established deadlines. 3. Grant Data & Pipeline Tracking Maintain 95% timely and accurate documentation of grant opportunities, proposals, awards, deadlines, reports, stewardship activities, and renewal opportunities within established Institutional Advancement tracking systems. 4. Grant Revenue & ROI Demonstrate measurable contribution toward Institutional Advancement fundraising goals by tracking and reporting dollars requested, dollars awarded, proposal success rate, grant revenue, renewals, and year-over-year performance. 5. Cross-Campus Collaboration Achieve 90% satisfaction from key internal partners, including Institutional Advancement, Finance, faculty, academic leadership, and program teams, through effective grant development support, communication, training, and collaboration. Essential Functions Strategic Grant Planning & Administration Develop and implement an annual and multi-year grant development strategy aligned with OLLU's institutional priorities, strategic plan, and fundraising goals. Establish annual goals for qualified opportunities, proposals submitted, dollars requested, awards secured, and grant renewals. Build and actively manage a comprehensive pipeline of public, private, corporate, foundation, federal, state, and local funding opportunities. Develop grant pipeline forecasts and provide regular progress reports to the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement. Track and analyze grant performance, success rates, revenue, and return on investment to inform future strategy. Maintain a comprehensive grants calendar incorporating prospecting, application, reporting, stewardship, and renewal deadlines. Develop a proactive institutional grant-development culture that generates fundable concepts before funding opportunities are released. Grant Prospect Research & Funding Intelligence Proactively identify and research grant-making organizations and funding opportunities that align with OLLU's mission, programs, academic priorities, and strategic initiatives. Utilize AI, grant databases, prospect research platforms, and emerging technologies to identify, evaluate, prioritize, and monitor funding opportunities. Monitor philanthropic, corporate, foundation, government, and higher education funding trends and communicate emerging opportunities to University leadership. Evaluate prospective funders for strategic alignment, funding capacity, historical giving, eligibility, and likelihood of support. Develop and maintain institutional funding intelligence to strengthen OLLU's long-term grant pipeline. Proposal Development & Submission Lead and coordinate the development of competitive grant proposals in collaboration with faculty, academic leaders, Finance, and program teams. Translate complex institutional priorities, academic programs, and community initiatives into compelling and fundable proposals. Review funding requirements, eligibility criteria, guidelines, and evaluation standards and develop clear proposal-development timelines. Coordinate proposal narratives, budgets, supporting documentation, institutional approvals, and required attachments. Guide proposals through the University's internal review and approval processes by facilitating grant concept development with faculty/program leaders; identifying projects with strong funding potential; developing concept papers/letters of inquiry; assessing project readiness before pursuing funding; helping departments articulate outcomes, evaluation plans, sustainability, and budgets. Ensure proposals are accurate, persuasive, compliant, and submitted by required deadlines. Develop and maintain standard proposal language, institutional data, templates, and other resources to improve efficiency and consistency. Funder Relationship Development & Cultivation In coordination with the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement, develop and strengthen relationships with foundation, corporate, government, and other institutional funders. Participate in funder meetings, briefings, site visits, cultivation activities, and stewardship efforts as appropriate. Research funder priorities and interests to strategically position OLLU for current and future funding opportunities. Identify opportunities to deepen institutional funder relationships and, when appropriate, transition relationships into broader philanthropic opportunities for the University. Coordinate funder relationship activity with Institutional Advancement to ensure consistent communication and relationship management. Strengthens the government grants and sponsored-program coordination in compliance with federal/state grant systems and registrations; government application requirements; indirect cost/F&A considerations; cost-sharing or matching requirements; subawards and external partners; institutional assurances and certifications; coordination with post-award/grants administration. Responsible for maintaining a funder portfolio and developing strategies such as: research ? introduction ? cultivation ? concept discussion ? proposal ? award ? stewardship ? renewal/upgrade. Cross-Campus Grant Development Serve as a strategic resource to faculty, administrators, and program leaders in identifying and developing fundable projects. Convene cross-functional teams for significant funding opportunities and establish responsibilities, timelines, budgets, and deliverables. Provide one-on-one consultation and technical assistance throughout the proposal-development process. Develop and deliver workshops and training on grant development, proposal writing, funder expectations, AI-assisted research, and grant best practices. Work closely with Finance and appropriate University offices to ensure proposed budgets and funding requests meet institutional and funder requirements. Build institutional capacity and a stronger culture of grant development across the University. Grant Stewardship, Reporting & Renewal Maintain a comprehensive schedule of grant reporting, stewardship, and renewal requirements. Coordinate with responsible departments to collect program outcomes, financial information, student impact, and other information required by funders. Facilitate timely submission of progress reports, final reports, and other required funder communications. Track funded projects and collaborate with appropriate University departments to support compliance with grant requirements. Identify successful grants for renewal, expansion, or development into larger funding opportunities. Collaborate with Institutional Advancement on appropriate recognition and stewardship of foundation, corporate, and institutional funders. Data, Reporting & Institutional Advancement Integration Maintain accurate and timely grant pipeline information within established Institutional Advancement systems. Track opportunities, submissions, dollars requested, awards, declines, success rates, reports, renewals, and projected revenue. Provide regular grant pipeline reports and forecasts to the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement. Collaborate with Advancement Operations to ensure appropriate grant and funder information is accurately maintained within Institutional Advancement systems. Use data and analytics to identify trends, evaluate performance, and inform future grant strategy. Contribute to overall Institutional Advancement planning and fundraising activities as a member of the IA team. Additional Responsibilities Participate as an active member of the Institutional Advancement team and support broader University fundraising and engagement initiatives as appropriate. Represent Institutional Advancement on University committees and working groups related to grant-funded initiatives. Maintain confidentiality and comply with university policies, funder requirements, and applicable regulations. Work occasional evenings and weekends, travel when necessary, and attend University and Institutional Advancement events Perform other duties as assigned by the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement. Requirements: Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Demonstrated knowledge of grant strategy, prospect research, proposal development, funder cultivation, stewardship, and grant reporting. Exceptional written communication and editing skills with the ability to develop clear, persuasive, and compelling proposals. Ability to translate complex academic, financial, and programmatic information into compelling funding narratives. Demonstrated ability to use AI and emerging technologies responsibly to enhance grant prospecting, research, proposal development, and funding intelligence. Knowledge of grant databases, research platforms, government grant systems, and related technology. Strong analytical skills and ability to use data to manage a grant pipeline and measure performance and ROI. Strong project-management skills with demonstrated ability to manage multiple complex proposals and deadlines. Ability to develop productive relationships with foundation, corporate, government, and institutional funders. Ability to work collaboratively with faculty, administrators, Finance, Institutional Advancement, and program teams. Strong presentation, consultation, facilitation, and training skills. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and take initiative. Ability to maintain confidentiality and adhere to the University's core values of Community, Integrity, Trust, and Service. Education and Experience Bachelor's degree required in communications, English, business, public administration, education, social sciences, or a related field; master's degree preferred. Requires a minimum of three to five years of progressively responsible experience in grant development, institutional fundraising, sponsored programs, foundation relations, or a related field. Demonstrated experience developing successful proposals for private foundations, corporate foundations, government agencies, or other institutional funders is required. Higher education or nonprofit grant development experience strongly preferred. Demonstrated experience managing multiple funding opportunities and complex proposal-development processes is required. Experience working collaboratively with faculty, senior leadership, Finance, and program teams preferred. Experience with grant research databases, electronic submission systems, CRM-Blackbaud (Raisers Edge) or grant-tracking systems, and Microsoft Office required. Demonstrated ability to incorporate technology, data, and AI-supported research into grant development strategy preferred. Additional Information: Employment with Our Lady of the Lake University is contingent upon the applicant undergoing a background investigation. Our Lady of the Lake University uses E-Verify to confirm the employment eligibility of all newly hired employees. To learn more about E-Verify, including your rights and responsibilities, please visit www.dhs.gov/E-Verify. OLLU seeks to attract, develop and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administrators. The University is committed to diversity and strongly encourages applications from women and minorities. EOE Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Our Lady of the Lake University 411 S.W. 24th Street\n\t\t\t\t\tSan Antonio, Texas 78207"
    },
    "https://ollusa.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=189787": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor of Mass Communication",
      "description": "Our Lady of the Lake University\nJob Posting: Adjunct Instructor of Mass Communication Our Lady of the Lake University \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Instructor of Mass Communication Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Instructor of Mass Communication Category: Adjunct Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: College of Arts & Sciences Locations: SAN ANTONIO, TX Posted: Jun 30, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 189787 Share About Our Lady of the Lake University: On a foundation of faith, wisdom and trust in Divine Providence, Our Lady of the Lake University (OLLU) empowers graduates to transform their communities, families, and professions.  Founded in 1895 by the Sisters of Divine Providence, OLLU stands as San Antonio's oldest regionally accredited institution of higher learning.  A private, Catholic university, it currently enrolls approximately 1,800 students across undergraduate and graduate programs. With its main residential campus in San Antonio and additional locations in Houston and the Rio Grande Valley (La Feria), and online programs, OLLU extends its mission across Texas, the border region, and the nation. Academic Programs OLLU offers a rich academic portfolio of more than 30 undergraduate majors and minors, 14 master's programs and three doctoral programs. Courses are delivered through diverse formats to support traditional and non-traditional students alike. Job Description: The Salazar-Escobedo School at Our Lady of the Lake University in San Antonio seeks an Adjunct Instructor of Mass Communication to be included in the general pool of candidates from which qualified part-time faculty will be selected for the upcoming academic term. The deadline for application is November 20 for consideration for the Spring semester and June 20 for the Fall semester. Responsibilities include: preparing and presenting course content under the direction of the Mass Communication program head; maintaining availability to students during the week (at least one hour per week per course taught); and participating in ongoing faculty development, which includes the completion of Blackboard Ultra workshops and training. The course(s) to be taught may include: COMM 2312 - Oral Communication Other courses related to the BA in Mass Communication and BA in Professional Writing and Communication, such as: · Introduction to Mass Media · Introduction to Film · Film Studies · Media Studies · Screenwriting · Public Relations. Requirements: Education & Experience: MA or MS in Mass Communication or related field required; PhD preferred. A demonstrated history of teaching Mass Communication courses and, ideally including other forms of technical and professional communication. (Relevant professional experience in the field will be considered in place of teaching history, provided advanced degree requirements are met.) Additional desirable qualifications include: · Professional experience in filmmaking and/or public relations · Ability to teach in person (on campus) preferred Additional Information: For questions about the position contact: Prof. Amanda Brown, Associate Professor of Practice in Mass Communications, aibrown@ollusa.edu, 210-431-5548. OLLU seeks to attract, develop and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administrators. The University is committed to diversity and strongly encourages applications from women and minorities. EOE. Employment with Our Lady of the Lake University is contingent upon the applicant undergoing a background investigation. Our Lady of the Lake University uses E-Verify to confirm the employment eligibility of all newly hired employees. To learn more about E-Verify, including your rights and responsibilities, please visit www.dhs.gov/E-Verify. Application Instructions: Please complete the online Application for Academic Employment and attach the following: Letter of Interest denoting position for which applying. Resume and/or Curriculum Vitae. Copies of all transcripts reflecting receipt of graduate degree in discipline for which applying. Listing of three professional references. For consideration for Academic Employment, all materials above must be submitted together and at one time. Incomplete packets for academic employment will not be considered. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Our Lady of the Lake University 411 S.W. 24th Street\n\t\t\t\t\tSan Antonio, Texas 78207"
    }
  },
